ich a code error hinders the program from performing well. Java uses exceptions in order to inform the programmer that something went wrong. It's curious, Java happens to see the error of its ways. <br>"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n5le02ob12re2img02.jpg", "time": "37000", "text": "<strong>Student: </strong>  I've talked with all programmers in the faculty and that's new for me. I thought that those errors were not able to be spotted. Tell me, how can programmers use those exceptions? <br>\n<strong>Teacher: </strong>   In Java, error handling needs specific words for the program to detect where the error may be. In this case, try and catch blocks are needed. See, you can foretell an error using a try block in which you write the code you need. After that, you use a catch block. The only way to know that there are no errors in the code is if the catch block does not function. If it works, that means that something went wrong. <br>"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n5le02ob12re2img03.jpg", "time": "80000", "text": "<strong>Student: </strong> What if there's no error? How can I cross out the catch block? <br>\n<strong>Teacher: </strong> You don't need to delete it, as it will disappear if the code works well. <br>\n<strong>Student: </strong>  I can see why try and catch blocks are so important; they can save programmers a great deal of time.<br>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le02ob12re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le02ob12re2aud01"}}]}, {"type": "video", "_data": [], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "id": 4, "contents": [{"type": "video", "value": {"video_src": "enpe7n5le02ob12re5vsc01", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "", "time": "0", "text": ""}]}}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}]}]}